  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none; full paper text is present.",
    "relation": "cites_only",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper cites the original grokking paper but does not test or rely on grokking. Its own experiments concern sequence-length extrapolation on dynamically generated state-tracking tasks, not delayed generalization after overfitting on a fixed small dataset.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports algorithmic-task length-generalization results and some CSP-Fast accuracy dynamics. These do not test the claim versions: sequences are dynamically regenerated, the main comparison is evaluation at longer sequence lengths, and the paper does not report a training-set-size sweep or establish that test accuracy rises after training accuracy saturates. The related length-generalization results are recorded under fits_no_version. Grokking appears in the references only.",
    "test_reason": "Grokking papers [32, 33] appear only in the reference list; the body never discusses them. The experiments measure length extrapolation on state-tracking tasks. Training data are regenerated every epoch, so no memorize-then-generalize phase can occur. There is no training-set-size sweep, no circuit analysis and no weight-norm or optimizer manipulation aimed at delayed generalization.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly correct. The authors also claim CSP-Vanilla beats Mamba and the Transformer on parity and mod-3 by '5–15 percentage points', which understates the Table 1 mod-3 margins (36–44). They say the Transformer is best on Dyck-1, and that CSP's rotation is numerically stable without eigenvalue clipping.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study trains on dynamically regenerated state-tracking sequences at length 16 and evaluates extrapolation to longer sequences; it does not reproduce the original fixed-small-dataset grokking setup.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The paper reports seed sensitivity for CSP, especially on parity and mod-3, and attributes it to initialization of the periodic input gate. CSP-Fast mod-3 accuracy is reported as perfect across evaluation lengths for three of five seeds, while other seeds plateau lower.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Models trained at length 16 split into tiers when evaluated up to length 64. Vanilla and complex RNNs stay near-perfect; CSP, Mamba and the Transformer decay with length. CSP results vary strongly by seed.",
        "proposed_claim": "Exact (unit-magnitude) state-transition architectures length-generalize on state-tracking tasks, while soft-contraction architectures decay with sequence length.",
